  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1976-p2.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Since \\((x+1)^2\\ge0,\\) its negative is at most \\(0.\\) It is nonnegative only when \\((x+1)^2=0,\\) which occurs for the single value \\(x=-1.\\)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "A real square root requires a nonnegative radicand",
        "The quantity \\(-(x+1)^2\\) can be nonnegative only when the square vanishes"
      ],
